<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maiduguri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yerwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maiduguri is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It grew from Yerwa, the new capital founded in 1907 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-garbai">Shehu Abubakar Garbai</a> of Borno under British rule, and remains the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shehu-of-borno">Shehu of Borno</a>. It is the largest city of north-eastern Nigeria and a trading centre for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maiduguri.jpg" alt="Maiduguri" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ifeatu Nnaobi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indimi_Mosque_Maiduguri_Borno_State_Nigeria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maiduguri lies along the seasonal Ngadda River, which disappears into the Firki swamps in the country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. The climate is hot and semi-arid, and the colonial administration made tree planting a priority, so that large trees along the main roads give shelter from the sun. The region had been home to the Kanem-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu Empire</a> for centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city consists of two parts: Yerwa to the west and Old Maiduguri to the east. Yerwa was founded in 1907 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-garbai">Abubakar Garbai</a> of Borno as the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukawa">Kukawa</a> as the capital of the Kanuri people. The site had been a small village called Kalwa. In 1908 the British chose Old Maiduguri, in place of Mafoni, as their military headquarters, and in the same year it became the seat of the British Resident Commissioner for British Bornu. In 1957 the name Yerwa was officially given to the urban centre and Maiduguri to the surrounding rural area. When the railway was extended to the city in 1964, it began to rise as a major commercial centre of the region.</p><p>Maiduguri has also been known as a centre of Islamic scholarship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Since the mid-1960s it has seen riots between religious groups, in 1982 and 2001 among others. In 2002 the cleric Mohammed Yusuf founded the group later known as Boko Haram there, with a mosque and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> that drew children from poor Muslim families in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and neighbouring countries. In 1975 the University of Maiduguri was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Maiduguri is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a> and is one of the local government areas of the traditional Borno Emirate. Its residents are mostly Muslim and include Kanuri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, Shuwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bura">Bura</a>, Marghi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a>, with a considerable Christian population and people from southern Nigerian states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a>, Ijaw and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>. A railway line links it with Port Harcourt, Enugu, Kafanchan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi">Bauchi</a>, and it has an international airport. It is the principal trading hub of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with an economy based mainly on services and trade, and has three large markets, among them the Monday Market. The city is also home to the El-Kanemi Warriors football club and to Kyarimi Park, the oldest and largest zoo in Nigeria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maiduguri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1907 he founded a new capital at <b>Yerwa</b>, which grew into the city of Maiduguri.</blockquote><a href="/state/borno-emirate">Borno Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
